WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:01.880
- [Sal] In this video,

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:05.770
we're going to talk a little
bit about ionic compounds,

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:07.960
and specifically we're
going to think about

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.870
the empirical formulas
for ionic compounds.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.730
So the first thing you
might be wondering is

00:00:12.730 --> 00:00:16.330
well, refresh me, Sal, on
what ionic compound is.

00:00:16.330 --> 00:00:17.920
Well, we're going to be dealing with ions,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.850
which you can view as the
charged versions of atoms.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.610
So, if you start with a neutral atom,

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:24.870
the only way it gets charged
is if you are gaining

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.030
or losing electrons, and
that would make it an ion.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:29.930
And then a compound, that tells us

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.330
that we're dealing with more
than one type of element.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:36.990
So let's just get a little
bit of a refresher here.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.820
So we've talked about this
in-depth in other videos,

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.180
but we know that atoms like to have

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:45.630
a full outer shell of electrons,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.690
a full number of valence electrons.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.840
Now, in most situations, that means

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:53.740
having eight valence electrons,

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.360
and that's why these noble
gases right over here

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.670
are considered to be so stable.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:00:59.380
They're already very satisfied.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.670
They have eight valence electrons.

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:03.350
They all have different
numbers of electrons,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:06.340
but the outer shell is eight.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.080
And the one exception
is when we're talking

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:08.913
about that first shell,

00:01:08.913 --> 00:01:10.882
and once again, this is all review

00:01:10.882 --> 00:01:12.246
that we've talked about in other videos.

00:01:12.246 --> 00:01:14.320
That first shell is most stable
when it has two electrons,

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:17.480
and that's why helium is also so stable.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.900
So in general, when atoms react,

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.570
they tend to react in a way that gets them

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:25.070
to either two electrons
in their first shell

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.910
or eight electrons in
any other outer shell,

00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:31.160
whichever one is their outermost shell.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.930
Now, how they get that, they can do that

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.020
in several different ways

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.410
and it really depends
on whether we're talking

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.530
about metals or non-metals.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.960
So first, let me separate
the non-metals here.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:41.830
So, hydrogen is a non-metal.

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.400
And then we'll come here

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:46.880
and this little staircase looking thing.

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.810
So everything to the right
and up of this dividing line

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.730
that I just drew, these
are all non-metals,

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:56.940
including hydrogen.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.380
Now, when non-metals interact

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.820
to try to get to that full outer shell,

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.370
they tend to do it by sharing
electrons with each other.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.950
For example, two hydrogens, each of which

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:11.010
have one outer electron,
well they can form

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:14.370
a covalent bond, which we
talk about in other videos,

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.280
and then they're each sharing
each other's electron,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.140
so they can kind of pretend like they have

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.130
a full outer shell.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.970
Now, it's different when we're
talking about a non-metal

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:27.130
interacting with a metal,
which we can generally refer to

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.720
the rest of the periodic table.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.250
There's other further sub-classifications.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:34.730
When you have a metal
interacting with a non-metal,

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.310
what typically happens
is that the non-metal

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.920
is actually going to gain electrons

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.290
to get to a full outer shell,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.830
while the metal will lose electrons

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:46.950
to get to a full outer shell.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:47.990
So I'll give you an example,

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:49.440
and I use this example a lot

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:52.320
and this is a common example
that gets us to table salt.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.000
If you look at sodium right over here,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.750
a neutral sodium atom
has one valence electron,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.200
one electron in that third shell.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.970
Well, the easiest way for it
to get to a full outer shell

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.240
is to give up that one electron,

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:12.040
and then it will have the same
number of electrons as neon.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.940
It still has 11 protons,
which make it a sodium,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.800
but it'll have the same
number of electrons as neon,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.300
and then it will be happy.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.270
So sodium tends to form

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:27.270
a sodium ion with a positive one charge.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.110
Now, we can go on the other
side of the periodic table.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:33.930
If we were to look at
chlorine right over here,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.850
chlorine has seven valence electrons.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:40.010
It has seven electrons
in that third shell.

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.630
Well, the easiest way for
it to get a full eight

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.750
is to nab an electron from someplace,

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.440
is to gain an electron.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.870
So chlorine tends to take
electrons from other atoms

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:55.870
and forms a negative charge
and becomes the chloride anion.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:57.830
Now, what do you think will happen

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.010
if you put a bunch of sodium

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.190
and a bunch of chlorine together?

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.930
Well, the chlorine's probably going to

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.820
take electrons from the sodium,

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.900
or the sodium is likely to
give electrons to the chlorine,

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:10.300
and then you have these two ions.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.290
One is positively charged,
one is negatively charged.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.240
They will be attracted to each other

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.240
and that's what forms the
ionic compound sodium chloride.

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:23.070
And this is the empirical
formula for table salt.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:26.330
For sodium chloride, for every sodium ion,

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:28.160
you have a chloride ion.

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:30.020
It's interesting to note that even though

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:31.840
the sodium has a positive charge,

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.980
the chlorine, which is
now the chloride anion

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:38.200
has a negative charge, that
the ionic compound in total

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:41.960
has a neutral charge, which
is an interesting property

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:43.887
of these ionic compounds.

